<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dikhow River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dikhou River</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Dikhow is a river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-assam">Upper Assam</a> in north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, a south-bank tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmaputra">Brahmaputra</a>. It rises in the hills of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagaland">Nagaland</a> and flows north-west through the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sivasagar">Sivasagar</a> to join the Brahmaputra. The early Ahom kingdom lay between the Dikhow and the Burhi Dihing, and the later Ahom heartland around Sivasagar, with its capitals, temples and royal tanks, lies along its lower course.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dikhow-river.jpg" alt="Dikhow River" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hiranmoy.boruah · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DIKHOW-BRIDGE.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
